{
  "dom_id": "step-e14047",
  "name": "Example: Identifying the Graph of a Non-Strict Inequality Given in Standard Form",
  "problem": "Sketch the solutions to the inequality $-x-y \\leq -1$ in the $xy$-plane.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x-y & \\le -1 \\\\ -y & \\le x-1 \\\\ y & \\ge -x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line \n\n$$\ny=-x+1.\n$$\n\n Since the inequality involves a 'greater than *or equal to*' sign, the line $y=-x+1$ itself is part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a *solid line*.\n\nThen, we must decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality \n\n$$\ny \\geq -x+1\n$$\n\n refers to the $y$-values that are *greater than* or equal to those on the line $y=-x+1,$ so we shade *above* the line. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/a649958285f52e9d61c665bf4cd58844e4ed7cea1cf9e0f949efe5a0f25e3263.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
